From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Fri, 9 Dec 1994 11:44:47 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-383] PHYS: New model for gravity

The most recent (12/3/94) issue of "Science News" contains an article
describing a new modification to General Relativity made by Huseyin Yilmaz
of Tufts University and Carroll Alley of the University of Maryland.  Among
other things, this new modification predicts:

  * An ether (I think), and
  * No black holes, no event horizons, no singularities.

I find these possibilities extremely intriguing for several reasons, including:

  * I have problems with both Newtonian and Einsteinian gravity,
  * The lack of black holes would substantially alter our current view
    of the universe.

On the subject of the latter: the concept of black holes has become so
assimilated into popular and scientific culture, even through there is
still no conclusive evidence for their existence.  It would be difficult to
find anyone on the street who has not heard of them, and they are used in
so many contemporary theories about the universe that they are essentially
accepted as fact by many prominent physicists, astronomers, and
cosmologists.

Lack of black holes would debunk many interesting, but highly speculative,
ideas: from "basement universes" to the "Omega point", and would seriously
embarrass many highly regarded scientists: including Hawking, Penrose, and
Tipler.  Not that I want to see these people derided; but this possibility
does remind me just how tenuous many ideas that are often taken for granted
can be.

One thing that I like about the new theory is that it allows free space
with a gravitational potential to make a contribution to the
stress-energy-momentum tensor.  I guess that the logic is something like
this: gravitational potential implies an energy density, which implies an
equivalent mass, which implies self-gravity of free space.  I don't know
what consequences this might have, but it would certainly affect
calculations concerning the currently disputed Hubble constant, existence
of dark matter, and global geometry/lifetime of the universe.

Apparently, Yilmaz and Alley are now doing laser time-of-flight experiments
to verify predictions their theory makes concerning the relative speed of
light in accelerated frames of reference, although I am unclear on how this
experiment is substantially different from that performed by Michelson and
Morley, which was published in 1887.  This certainly sounds like an
ether-related experiment, however.

I wonder how this theory, if verified, will affect ideas like entropy,
negative matter, and traversable wormholes?

--Vince

