From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Fri, 9 Dec 1994 11:44:24 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-384] SING: Intelligence, etc...

John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us> sez:

>I don't think he could ever understand how his mind works well
>enough to improve it or make an agent that could ,regardless of
>how much time he had . To do that, intelligence must pass a
>threshold of complexity, humans have passed that point ,dogs
>alas have not.

While I agree that humans seem to be more intelligent than dogs and other
mammals, I'm not sure that this can be quantified simply in terms of
complexity or in speed of operation.  This kind of antinomy can be very
misleading: just how many neurons does it take to make one intelligent or
self-aware?  What kind of a "threshold" are you suggesting, and what
evidence do you have to support it?

>2) There's no point in talking about things that are
>unimaginable, like a mind immensely more powerful than ours not
>just faster. I would also put the world after the singularity in
>this category. Undoubtedly there will be huge differences in the
>world a minute, a day , a year and a million years after the
>singularity but I haven't a clue about what those differences might be.

I agree, which is why I have been very skeptical about the concept of the
singularity.  However, I still don't see that the world will be so
substantially different after any particular technological event.  For
example, while the world is certainly a better and more interesting place
since the invention of the transistor, it is by no means unimaginable to
someone living before 1957.  In fact, I think there is a historical
precedent for technological innovation and its consequences to underachieve
speculation: consider the pre-1950 predictions of "the world of the
future", including colonies on the Moon and flying cars.

hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson) sez:

>It seems the name "horizon" captures what most people mean, a time
>beyond which its hard to project from now.  "Singularity" shoould be
>used by those who do project a certain special moment in time or
>maximal change.

This is a very reasonable assessment.  It seems to me that there will be a
continuous series of such horizons, depending of the specific technology in
question.  If one could show that the projected occurrences of these
individual horizons are all converging on a certain moment in time, then
that moment would potentially qualify as a singularity.

Jordan Sparks <sparksj@ucs.orst.edu> sez:

>We were obviously optimized for this time frame and would go
>insane if the world (from our perspective) slowed down by a factor of
>millions.

I don't see this at all.  There are many interesting phenomena which occur
at factors of a million times our current perceptual rate.  While the world
would certainly look different to us with such a speedup, I don't think it
would be different in any non-linear fashion.  As to our being optimized in
any way at all, I can think of no evolutionary evidence for this.  Our
time-base is probably simply a consequence of the signal-carrying physical
properties of the raw materials which were around when our neural system
began to form.

>Now, if you increase the speed of the human's
>mind (say, double it), the VR has to keep up by doubling its speed.  The
>relationship between the speed of the VR and the human brain is a
>constant, no matter what the factor of increase in speed up.

This is a very interesting thought!  The primary reason that computers are
so important to us is not that they can do anything new, but that they can
automate what would otherwise be manual tasks, and speed them up by
significant orders of magnitude.  If we were "running" at essentially the
same speed as our computers, there would be no perceptual difference
between the two, and computers might cease to be of significant use to us.
This could essentially take us back to the "pencil and paper" era for all
calculations, irrespective of how fast that pencil and paper might be.

John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us> sez:

>What unique characteristic do events in "this" time frame have
>that we are optimized for, and why don't other time frames have
>it as well ? Nature optimized us to solve problems, the time
>frame is irrelevant.

I agree completely (with, perhaps, exception to the "intent" of Nature).

nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu (Nancy Lebovitz) sez:

>I think that a billion-fold increase in thinking would be enough to
>create a difference in kind

>There are a lot of cases where a difference in degree can lead to
>a difference in kind. A star is wildly different from a hydrogen/helium
>mixture in a tank.

I understand what motivates you to think this, but I don't see a logical
rationale for this.  I think it is necessary to identify both the point at
which such a dynamical bifurcation occurs, as well as the underlying cause
of such an event.  What is it about the dynamics of a system that is so
different simply when a parameter such as timescale is changed?  I do agree
with your law that "things don't always scale", and there are many cases in
which an incremental change in one system parameter causes very non-linear
changes in other parameters.  However, I think it is necessary to identify
the mechanisms which relate these parameters.  So far, I have seen no such
mechanisms for the speed/intelligence connection.

>For that matter, associational ability might need to be in an
>optimal middle range--too little, and the rest of one's knowledge
>doesn't get involved in the new idea (either to check it or to
>expand it)--too much, and there's such a flood of association
>that there's no way to check on whether any of it makes sense.

>Also, there are some other essential abilities that I don't
>have names for--the ability to check one idea against another
>and the ability to keep one's memories organized.

These ideas make intuitive sense as well, but for what reason?  I guess I
would like to see evidence for this hypothesis, perhaps in the form of a
series of AI simulations in which these factors (memory retention and
fidelity, degree of association between keys and data, etc...) are
optimized in a competetive problem-solving environment, perhaps by a set of
genetic algorithms.

>I'm willing to
>argue that people in inner cities are under much more evolutionary
>pressure than most of us, and might, for example, be evolving the
>ability to maintain intelligence on fewer nutriants.

I think the actual situation is just the opposite.  I think we are
significantly eliminating selective pressures by our artificial support of
competetively sub-optimaly-motivated and skill-set-lacking individuals.  In
addition, we are artificially augmenting their fecundity (reproductive
rate) without a concomitant return of value.  If we continue these
policies, we run the risk of their substantially out-breeding us, which is
effectively cutting our own throats.  My personal opinions aside, I think
that the situation you cite is one in which the *least* amount of evolution
is occurring.

>I was assuming that intelligence is (at least partly) biological, but
>that there might be a way to increase it (perhaps with supplements)
>through technology. (In fact, caffeine may be a way of getting a
>small intelligence increase.)

This last part is from an L. Neil Smith book, isn't it ("The Nagasaki
Vector")?  I agree that anything that facilitates successful
problem-solving experience also increases intelligence.  However, remember
that the increase of intelligence of any individual organism during its
lifetime is not passed on to its progeny.  Those individuals with a
biological predilection for such a process (whether they exercise it or
not), and who can and choose to reproduce at a greater than average rate,
will effect an increase in intelligence of the overall population.

>I've wondered if groups with, say, differences
>of 5 points in the IQ of their typical members behave differently.
>My bet is that they do, and that a smallish general increase (which
>also happens at the top end) might cause rapid enough bootstrapping
>(more intelligence leads to the ability to generate yet more) to
>count as a singularity.

What you're talking about is a positive feedback loop.  While I agree that
this process is plausible, I doubt that the real systems we're considering
represent valid examples of such a simple first-order dynamic.  I won't say
that "all systems have limits", but it does seem to be true that most
natural systems involve damping, feedback delays, and other mechanisms
which prevent unbounded behavior.  Another approach is to look at this idea
in an evolutionary sense: those systems for which unbounded positive
behavior is possible may have already done so long ago, leaving us only
with systems which are naturally bounded.  Potentially unbounded systems
may already have had their "singularities".  In any case, even unbounded
positive feedback does not imply a singularity: there are lots of real
numbers out there, and they go on and on...

dasher@netcom.com (Anton Sherwood) sez:

>It might even be
>feasible to isolate the effects of your favorite drugs on various _parts_
>of your brain, and control them with handy switches.

I would very much like to imprint some of my most memorable, er,
"experiences" (of all sorts), onto a net for replay over and over again.
However, I'm sure that the FDA would step in at that point.

>Logical chunking is an interesting problem.  Are there clusters of neurons
>such that each member's output function is smooth and monotone, but the
>cluster's output function is chaotic?

The answer to this is probably "yes", but I don't think there is any
rhyme-or-reason to our understanding of this phenomenon at present.  While,
in general, the output of groups of neurons is "smoother" than that of an
individual neuron, this is probably just an artifact of signal averaging.
I realize that you're hypothesizing the reverse, but at present, we don't
even know what the real information content of a neural signal is: some
neurons appear to be voltage-based, some frequency-based.  It has even
recently been speculated (in this month's "Physics Today") that they might
employ some kind of binary digital encoding.  Until we can interpret the
output of a single neuron better than we do now, it makes little sense to
compare it to the activity of a tissue as a whole.

>It's fairly simple,
>in concept, to make replacement neurons that mimic your brain's function at
>this moment; much harder to get them to make new connections.

Agreed. This will be an essential problem for uploaded nets: how to
continue making new connections, or at least altering the relative
strengths of existing connections.  In effect, this means that the nets
will have to be continually "training", just as our own brains are.
However, note that new neural connections are formed primarily in juvenile
brains, and that such nets may contain twice the neurons (which eventually
die off) as do adults.

nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu (Nancy Lebovitz) sez:

>I seriously doubt that the first uploads will have a billion-fold speedup.
>It's possible that the first uploads will be slower than flesh humans.
>I'd assume that acceleration will be a matter of experiment.

My point exactly.

>I hope that uploads would also be able to program their own VRs.

I wonder, however, if they'll be able to program their VCR's :-).

--Vince

