From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 09 Dec 94 15:16:09 EST
Subject: [#94-12-386] SING: Post-singularity morals

Phil Goetz wrote 12/9/94: <Post-singularity, intelligence will exist on a
continuum.  We will have to confront the problem of moral relations between
creatures of different levels of intelligence.>

I can't see any methodological change in these from where we are now.  Just
different scales.

<I think that if these memes are dominant post-Singularity, the Universe will
either (1.) be filled with unrelenting war between all manners of beings,
forever, or (2.) be the private preserve of a few gods.>

(2.) will fall to the attractor of (1.).  But remember that war is a continuum,
too.  Most of the time, war is uneventful, peace is very slow war.

<Extropians, I suspect, don't fret over this too much because they hope to be
gods.  They assume that everyone will want to increase their intelligence
indefinitely, and so no one would willingly occupy the lower levels anyway.>

The question is, why would they want this?  The answer is, if they want to
maintain their self-determination.  It really has little to do with happiness,
enjoyment or any other motivation.

<Also, we get for free a dictum against violence:  destruction increases
entropy, which is the inverse of complexity.>

But capitalism is "creative destruction," if nothing were ever destroyed, we
would be in the neo-Gnostic physicist's heaven, thermodynamic heat death of the
universe.

<Individuals possess rights.  But, post-singularity, drawing the lines around
individuals may be hard.>

Who granted the individuals rights?  Who enforced them?  Who draws the lines
around individuals now?  What will change from now?

<When intelligences join together in networks, and those networks into larger
networks, is each level an individual, and does it have the right to suppress
its constituent individuals?>

A colony of sponges is "a" sponge, as far as smarts go.  Who would grant it
rights?  Who would enforce them?

<Can it allow its constituent individuals to violate each others' rights, as we
may suppress parts of our personality?>

Why not?  Who granted these rights anyway?  Who enforces them?

<If you have five daemons in your mind, and you suppress one, is it oppression?>

E-mail this question to Bill Clinton for a testimonial.

<If you join a collective personality, and it decides to remake you, is it
murder?>

Legally?  Who makes the laws?  Who enforces them?  Who decided you could join?
Who decided on the terms and interpretation of your association?

<What if you have partial membership in several larger individuals?>

Like a parasitical relationship?  Or like club memberships?  Who decides to have
you and what terms?  Who interprets the terms over time?

<What if "you" share your memories with others (possibly for a price), and "you"
gain some of their memories -- if you go far enough in that direction, you will
come to the point where your mental content is not concentrated in any one
information locus identifiable as "you".>

Never trust someone else's memories, you know how garbled your own are.  If you
do mind-meld, well, self-immolation is a time-honored mode of worshipping death.

